00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:42.420
Liubov's testimony and songs

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.700
offer important insight into some

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:46.700
lesser known aspects of the history

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:47.980
of the Holocaust.

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:50.166
While teeming concentration camps,

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:52.640
gas chambers, and

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.860
industrial crematoria still

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:57.349
dominate popular imagery

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:58.990
of the Holocaust, Liubov's

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:01.780
testimony is more representative

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.210
of the experience of Soviet Jews,

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:06.939
who, by and large, were not deported

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:08.165
to the killing centers in

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:09.910
German-occupied Poland.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:12.969
Instead, in the small towns and

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.420
cities of Ukraine, Belarus,

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:17.620
the Baltic states and western

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.989
Russia, many able bodied

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:22.029
Jewish men, young children

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:23.549
and elderly fell victim to mass

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:25.774
shootings during the early months of

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:27.149
the German occupation.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:29.686
Surviving adults were condemned to

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:32.379
forced labor in the ghettos or small

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.060
labor camps, living in

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:35.860
constant fear of

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:37.720
ongoing mass shootings.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:40.537
Liubov's testimony highlights the

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:42.900
experience of Jews in the small,

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:45.019
virtually unknown ghettos

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:47.140
and camps, where grueling labor

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.095
and appalling living conditions led

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:52.329
to high rates of injury, malnutrition

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:53.330
and disease.

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:56.229
Budyshche was the third camp

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:58.509
in a series of labor camps where

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:00.519
German overseers and

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.559
local politsai transported

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:04.689
Liubov' and her fellow prisoners

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.449
as slave laborers

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:09.850
for the Transit Highway IV project

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:12.249
between May 1942

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:14.440
and August 1943.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:17.264
While these Jewish laborers were, in

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:19.539
theory, incredibly valuable

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:21.240
for the German war effort,

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:23.287
the Nazi regime allowed so-called

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:25.780
accidental deaths to claim

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:27.100
many lives.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.149
And the local gendarmerie added

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:31.709
to the death toll with punitive

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:34.260
shootings and camp liquidations.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:36.843
Of the four songs that Liubov' K.

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:38.887
shares in her testimony,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:41.850
"Tuchi nad Budishchem Vstali" offers

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.568
the most comprehensive summation

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.169
of day to day life in the small,

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.779
improvised labor camps along

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:49.780
the Transit Highway IV.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.661
It recounts the frigid winter

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.139
conditions in the unheated

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:57.510
clubhouse where prisoners were housed.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.104
It describes backbreaking labor

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:02.639
they performed on the transit highway

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:04.595
and their efforts to supplement the

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:07.019
meager food rations with

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.029
food from the local villagers.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:11.909
It also references the threat of the

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:13.235
cruel guard Stepan,

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:15.654
who meted out punishment for running

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.009
off to the village.

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.549
Despite the song's somber

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:21.820
lyrics, Liubov'

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:25.049
takes visible joy in performing

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:26.716
it for the interviewers during her

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:28.090
testimony.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:30.539
As they composed the song in the

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:32.669
camps, Liubov' and her fellow

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.780
prisoners borrowed the lilting

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:37.290
melody of a popular Soviet song,

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:39.740
Tuchi nad Gorodom Vstali,

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.119
from the 1938 movie

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:43.339
Man with a Gun.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.464
The fact that the original performer

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.140
of the song, Mark Bernes

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.429
was a Ukrainian Jew was

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:52.379
no doubt a point of great pride

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:54.930
for these prisoners and the melody

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.577
a welcome reminder of their lives

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:57.910
before the war.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:00.600
For these reasons, perhaps,

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:02.780
the song ends with a confident

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:04.686
assertion that the tide of the war

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.129
will turn, victory

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:09.139
will come, and joy

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:11.170
will be possible once again.

